After completing this tutorial, you should be able to:

distinguish an R package from its upstream data provider;
compare official APIs, maintained repositories, package wrappers, and HTML scraping;
identify widely used R packages for major sports;

6.1 Inspect the data dictionary
Variable names such as home_wp, wp, and vegas_wp may look similar but have different definitions. Use the package data dictionary rather than guessing from a name.

nflreadr::dictionary_pbp %&gt;%
  filter(Field %in% c("home_wp", "wp", "vegas_wp")) %&gt;%
  select(Field, Description)

Before modeling, check:

the unit of observation;
the exact outcome definition;
whether a field is known at prediction time;
whether a value was calculated after the event;
the missing-value convention; and
whether team and player identifiers are stable across seasons.

After completing this tutorial, you should be able to:

use R for arithmetic and vectorized calculations;
distinguish vectors, factors, lists, matrices, arrays, data frames, and tibbles;
extract values with [, [[, and $;
calculate a transpose, determinant, inverse, and matrix product;
solve a system of linear equations and connect matrix algebra to regression;
manipulate a sports data frame with core dplyr verbs; and
write a readable pipeline with %&gt;% and handle missing values explicitly.

After completing this tutorial, you should be able to:

explain the difference between HTML scraping and an API request;
map JSON values to R data structures;
build an HTTP request with query parameters;
inspect and flatten nested JSON without losing record relationships;
check HTTP and data-level errors;
write a reusable API function; and
handle authentication, pagination, rate limits, and caching responsibly.
